WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.982
Right-sizing means matching the resource to the workload instead of starting from the largest familiar option.

00:00:05.982 --> 00:00:10.026
GPU type, GPU count, accelerator memory, CPU, system memory, local storage,

00:00:10.026 --> 00:00:14.805
remote storage throughput, and network capacity all affect whether a job runs efficiently.

00:00:14.805 --> 00:00:18.481
Memory fit is often as important as raw accelerator performance.

00:00:18.481 --> 00:00:22.158
If the model, batch, or dataset pipeline does not fit,

00:00:22.158 --> 00:00:25.466
the job may fail or require a different configuration.

00:00:25.466 --> 00:00:29.878
If the GPU is powerful but waits on CPU preprocessing, slow storage,

00:00:29.878 --> 00:00:35.025
or network transfer, the expensive part of the system is not doing useful work.

00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:37.966
Utilization metrics help teams see what is happening.

00:00:37.966 --> 00:00:42.009
GPU utilization, memory usage, queue wait time, job duration, failure rate,

00:00:42.009 --> 00:00:46.421
and data loading behavior can show whether the workload is compute-bound, memory-bound,

00:00:46.421 --> 00:00:47.891
input-bound, or simply oversized.

00:00:47.891 --> 00:00:51.568
For early experiments, smaller or fewer GPUs may be enough.

00:00:51.568 --> 00:00:55.244
A team can test code paths, validate data, confirm configuration,

00:00:55.244 --> 00:00:58.553
and estimate scaling behavior before moving to larger capacity.

00:00:58.553 --> 00:01:02.229
That pattern reduces failed large jobs and makes learning cheaper.

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:04.435
Right-sizing is not a one-time choice.

00:01:04.435 --> 00:01:08.846
Models, datasets, batch sizes, traffic patterns, runtime libraries, and business needs change.

00:01:08.846 --> 00:01:14.361
A resource that was a good match last month may be wasteful or insufficient later.

00:01:14.361 --> 00:01:17.302
Regular review keeps the allocation connected to reality.

00:01:17.302 --> 00:01:20.978
A useful right-sizing practice records the reason for the choice.

00:01:20.978 --> 00:01:25.757
If a job needs a larger GPU because of memory, write that down.

00:01:25.757 --> 00:01:30.168
If it needs more CPU because preprocessing is heavy, record that too.

00:01:30.168 --> 00:01:34.580
Future maintainers can then revisit the decision with evidence instead of guessing.
